# Lore.Land Spw stress
# What the site has learned about the language by running on it: Exactly One Pie reads its
# rules (.spw/runtime/pie-rules.spw) with the vendored parser at runtime, compares that read
# with its own small reader, round-trips every power, and fuzzes the rules on purpose
# (book/scripts/home/pie-spw.mjs; the findings panel at /pie/#pie-stress). Each finding here is
# meant to travel upstream to spw-workbench as an issue or a proposal.
#>lore_land_surfaces_spw_stress
#:surface #!audit
#:layer #!pragmatics
#:status #!live

~<rationale>{
  A language earns trust by being leaned on. The game leans on Spw for real things: tuning how the lanes feel, what each knock costs, what each gift does and which references it calls on. When the parser and the small reader agree, and every power survives a round trip, the grammar is carrying weight. When they do not, or when a broken spell is called a success, that is worth knowing before a reader finds it.
}

?findings[
  quiet_prose_fallback: { measured: "2026-11-08, spw-seed edbc550, lexProfile prose" seen: "a dropped closing brace, a dropped opening bracket, or an emoji in a label: parse() returns success true, errors 0, warnings 1, with completeness.complete false and completeness.proseFallback true" why: "a consumer that checks success alone takes a broken rule set as read" proposal: "under a structured reading, report success false (or a structural flag) when the root falls back to prose; or emit an error rather than a warning" }
  unclosed_string_cliff: { measured: "5.6 KB of rules" seen: "an unclosed string inside #says[...] takes about 1 s to parse, against about 0.3 s for the whole file read clean" why: "one typo on the bench stalls the page" proposal: "stop a string at the end of the line (strings are one line in this canon already), then recover" }
  cold_parse_cost: { measured: "node 22 and Chrome, cold" seen: "about 300 to 430 ms for the 5.6 KB rules file; 10 to 40 ms for a single 100-character spell; the bundle was 634 KB, and since 2026-11-10 is 174 KB (42 KB gzip), tree-shaken to the names lore.land imports; 219 canon samples and 269 bias edges read identically before and after; parse time did not change, and the rules file, now 9.7 KB, takes about 0.6 to 0.8 s in node" why: "a runtime reader has to stay off the first paint (the game reads its rules when the page is idle)" proposal: "export a smaller runtime entry (lite.ts exists) for reading rules, without the whole workbench" }
  reference_frame_asymmetry: { seen: "@voice[wonder] parses as Reference { raw: voice } with the @ sigil not kept on the node, and its frame on the enclosing Expression; ~lit[7] parses as Operation { operator, operatorLabel, frame } with the frame on the term" why: "a walker needs a special case for @, and a reader cannot tell @voice[x] from voice[x] by the node alone" proposal: "keep the sigil on Reference nodes (perspective prefix), and attach the frame to the same term as for operations" }
  valence_words_reserved: { seen: "!honk parses as an Operation with no label and a ModifierChain [honk]; [boon] or [bone] inside a frame parse as an empty Expression carrying a ModifierChain" why: "everyday words of this book (and of the game: a goose honks, a berry is a boneberry) cannot be labels or values without a workaround" proposal: "the upstream change already under way: let a valence word be an identifier where an identifier is expected, and a modifier only where a modifier can stand" answered: "in part, at workbench f143ba31 (re-tested 2026-11-10 at be2df2f): a valence word before a colon is a key and inside a hyphenated name is part of the name; lint warnings across this tree fell from 18 to 11. !honk and [honk] in a frame stay charges, which valence_words_are_modifiers says to keep" }
  silent_acceptance: { seen: "a doubled operator (~~lit[7]) and a number with two points (1.0.5) both read whole, with no warnings" why: "a tuning typo changes the game without anyone being told" proposal: "warn on a doubled sigil and on a malformed number literal" }
  probe_list_newline: { seen: "?name[ … ] and #name[ … ] (and ?name{ … } bodies) read whole on one line but degrade the whole surface to prose when they span lines; the other ten sigils read the same multi-line list or body whole (found three times: this file as ?findings, PLAN.F as ?open, and dragon-egg.spw as ?probes, the pattern .spw/CLAUDE.md recommends; a bare ?name[ … ] without quotes reads whole)" why: "found by writing this file as ?findings[ … ] (now %findings, a measure); the probe and the annotation are the two sigils a findings list reaches for first" proposal: "let ? and # open a multi-line frame like the others, or warn with the line and the sigil" }
  postfix_observer_detached: { seen: "a room signature ~{warm_furrow}@boof parses as two expressions side by side, the operation and a separate Reference; the postfix @ observer is not attached to what it observes" why: "found writing 81 room signatures; the observer reading is a convention of the reader, not of the tree" proposal: "attach a postfix @ to the expression it follows, as the spirit sequence describes" }
  resolve_html_anchors: { measured: "2026-11-10, workbench be2df2f" seen: "spw resolve reports 20 missing anchors, most in pie-rules.spw, for refs like book/chapter/02#what-the-echo-keeps; the ids are in the built pages" why: "the resolver reads .spw targets and cannot see an id in generated HTML, so a route with a fragment looks broken" proposal: "classify a fragment on a route as unverified rather than missing, or let a consumer declare where its anchors come from" }
  negative_frame_value: { seen: "a negative number in a frame (%delay[-4]) stops the structured read at the next block and degrades the whole surface to prose, with one warning far from the cause" why: "found 2026-11-09 writing .spw/runtime/audio-tropes.spw: a canon that leads instead of follows wanted a delay below zero; written as %lead[4] instead" proposal: "lex a leading minus on a number literal in a frame, or name the token and line in the warning" }
  valence_words_are_modifiers: { seen: "bone boon bane bonk honk inside a frame read as modifiers on an empty expression, not as names: !voice[honk] has no identifier in it" why: "found 2026-11-09 by book:check holding the game reader of pie-levels.spw to the interpreter; the reader now takes a lone modifier as the value" proposal: "keep it (the valence words are grammar), and say so in the frame reading docs" }
  silent_swallow: { seen: "?<…>, #(…) and #<…> return success with the operator swallowing the rest of the line as prose; no room is in the tree" why: "book:check now checks the shape of the tree, not success, and refuses those pairs" proposal: "the same as quiet_prose_fallback: a structured read that does not find structure should say so" }
  capsule_attachment_varies: { seen: "before < >, the sigils & ! . = * % $ @ make a capsule standing between the operator and what follows, while ~ and ^ make a capsule held by the operation; after an operator ( ) hangs on the expression, where [ ] and { } hang on the operation; a capsule stores its name twice, as tag and as channel" why: "a walker needs a case for each pairing of sigil and container" proposal: "one attachment rule for all four containers after any operator" }
  gaps_meaning: { seen: "a clean, complete read of the rules reports 913 gaps" why: "the number is not interpretable from outside, so it cannot be used as a health measure" proposal: "document what a gap is, or report gaps that matter (unread spans) separately from whitespace" }
  bias_anchor_after_frame: { measured: "2026-11-08, spw-seed edbc550, lexProfile prose" seen: "=meme[pov]@boof{ pop } reads complete with no warnings as two expressions: =meme[pov] with no body, and @boof carrying the body on its Expression; readBias() returns null for it. =meme@boof[pov]{ pop } reads as one edge with anchor boof and axis pov" why: "found writing the meme registers (.spw/runtime/bias.spw); the bias product lists the anchored and axial forms apart and never together, so the order a writer reaches for first (frame, then who) silently loses the edge" proposal: "state the order in bias-product.spw (label, anchor, valence, frame, body), and warn when an = with a frame and no body is followed at once by @name{ … }; or attach the body to the operation, as attach_rules asks" }
  bias_label_sign_order: { seen: "a labeled, signed edge reads only as =meme bane[pov]{ pop } (label, a space, the valence word); =bane meme[pov]{ pop } reads complete as =<bane> with no body beside an Identifier meme holding the frame and body, and readBias() returns null; the registry shows only =bane@old{ @new }, with the sign in label place" why: "a reader-cast register wants both a consumer label (meme) and a sign; spw-bias.mjs writes the signed form unlabeled (=bane[pov]{ pop }) to stay readable" proposal: "give the valence one written place relative to the label (=meme.bane[…] or =bane:meme[…]) and document it beside the five forms" }
  bias_body_measures: { seen: "readBias() keeps only scalar targets from a body, so =meme[pov]{ pop %often[0.5] } reports targets [pop] and drops the measure; a consumer walks the body again to find %often" why: "a lean often carries how strongly it leans; every consumer of a weighted edge repeats the same walk" proposal: "report the body operations readBias() skips as params (a sigil, a label and its frame values), still verb-neutral" }
  ground_before_capsule: { measured: "2026-11-11, spw-seed vendored bundle, both lex profiles" seen: ".<boon> reads complete as a bare Capsule: the ground sigil is dropped before <, with no warning; .(boon) and .[boon] keep it as an Operation" why: "found writing the container ladder (formulas.spw): grounding a concept one step, from <boon> down to (boon), has no form the tree keeps, while binding up (^<boon>, ^{boon}) does" proposal: "tokenize . as ground before every container, or warn when a sigil is dropped" }
  reference_body: { measured: "2026-11-11" seen: "@boof{ <boon>{bone} } reads complete as a Reference with the body hung on its Expression, as reference_frame_asymmetry found for frames" why: "a mind stated as Spw (formulas.spw, minds) needs its body; spw-formulas.mjs readMind walks the Expression to find it" proposal: "the same as reference_frame_asymmetry: keep the sigil on the Reference and hang the body on the same term" }
]

^"how_the_game_tests"{
  reads: "pie-spw.mjs readRules(): the real parser (lazily imported), walked into tune, knocks and powers; the small reader in pie-powers.mjs when the parser cannot be had or does not read the rules whole"
  compares: "both readers build powers through one buildPower(), so a difference between them is a difference in reading; each power is written back out (writePower) and read again"
  fuzzes: "eight mutations: a dropped brace, a dropped bracket, an unclosed string, a swapped sigil, a doubled operator, a malformed number, an emoji in a label, an apostrophe in prose"
  reports: "stressReport() writes ?stress[exactly_one_pie]{ %parse quirks cross fuzz } for the workbench"
  dispatches: "the rules call on references the game acts on: ^card turns a card, !sound picks a sound, @voice colours the caption, and a quoted path becomes a link to where the book has it"
}
